WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.816
Insertion sort builds its answer

00:00:02.895 --> 00:00:04.625
one element at a time.

00:00:04.625 --> 00:00:06.984
Merge sort uses another idea

00:00:06.984 --> 00:00:08.557
divide-and-conquer

00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:11.781
which is usually written with recursion.

00:00:11.781 --> 00:00:14.612
Let's meet recursion with a puzzle

00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:16.185
the Tower of Hanoi.

00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:19.566
Three towers, A, B and C, and three disks

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:22.948
all on tower A, the largest at the bottom.

00:00:22.948 --> 00:00:25.700
The goal is to move them all to C

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.194
under three rules.

00:00:27.194 --> 00:00:29.553
Move only one disk at a time.

00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:32.935
Only the top disk of a tower may be moved.

00:00:33.014 --> 00:00:35.294
And never place a larger disk

00:00:35.373 --> 00:00:37.260
on top of a smaller one.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.159
How would you do it

00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:41.714
and how many moves does it take?

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:43.650
Pause the video and try.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.048
Here is a solution by recursion.

00:00:50.048 --> 00:00:52.954
To move 3 disks from A to C

00:00:52.954 --> 00:00:56.124
first move the top 2 from A to B

00:00:56.124 --> 00:00:58.590
using C as the spare tower.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.109
Disk 1 to C, disk 2 to B

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:05.060
and disk 1 back onto it.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.347
Now the largest disk moves from A to C.

00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:11.463
Tower C is empty, so it breaks no rule

00:01:11.463 --> 00:01:14.422
and afterwards it sits at the bottom

00:01:14.422 --> 00:01:17.070
where nothing is smaller than it.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.342
Finally, move the 2 disks from B to C

00:01:21.342 --> 00:01:24.970
using A as the spare: disk 1 to A

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.500
disk 2 to C, and disk 1 on top.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.778
7 moves.

00:01:29.778 --> 00:01:33.462
The 2 middle-sized jobs, move 2 disks

00:01:33.462 --> 00:01:36.974
are the same puzzle with 1 disk fewer

00:01:36.974 --> 00:01:39.630
so we solve them the same way.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:43.139
In Java, hanoi moves n disks from

00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:45.180
the tower called from

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.488
onto the tower called to

00:01:47.488 --> 00:01:49.618
using via as the spare.

00:01:49.618 --> 00:01:52.546
With 1 disk, it just moves it.

00:01:52.635 --> 00:01:54.943
Otherwise it moves n - 1 disks

00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:57.250
onto the spare

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.822
moves disk n

00:01:58.916 --> 00:02:02.301
and moves the n - 1 disks from

00:02:02.395 --> 00:02:04.840
the spare onto the target.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.654
Let T(n) be the number

00:02:07.757 --> 00:02:09.913
of moves for n disks.

00:02:09.913 --> 00:02:12.172
Then T(1) is 1

00:02:12.275 --> 00:02:15.048
and T(n) is 1 more than

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.512
twice T(n - 1).

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:22.030
The solution is 1 less than 2 to the n.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.213
That is 7 moves for 3 disks

00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:28.282
1023 for 10

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.957
and more than 10 to the 18

00:02:31.036 --> 00:02:32.216
for 64.

00:02:32.295 --> 00:02:34.655
Lecture 6 shows how to solve

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:36.780
recurrences like this one.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.282
Hanoi is an example of divide-and-conquer

00:02:40.282 --> 00:02:42.995
which solves a problem in three steps.

00:02:43.065 --> 00:02:46.126
Divide the problem into subproblems that are

00:02:46.196 --> 00:02:48.839
smaller instances of the same problem.

00:02:48.839 --> 00:02:51.761
Conquer them by solving them recursively.

00:02:51.761 --> 00:02:54.196
When a subproblem is small enough

00:02:54.196 --> 00:02:56.840
it is a base case: solve it directly.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.820
Then combine their solutions into a

00:02:59.896 --> 00:03:02.376
solution of the original problem.

00:03:02.376 --> 00:03:05.757
Picture it as a diamond: one problem splits

00:03:05.757 --> 00:03:07.411
the parts are solved

00:03:07.411 --> 00:03:10.266
and the solutions flow back together.

00:03:10.342 --> 00:03:12.746
Divide-and-conquer is most often

00:03:12.822 --> 00:03:14.550
written with recursion.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.187
Recursion is self-reference

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.537
a recursive function is defined

00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:21.006
in terms of itself.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.503
The solution of the whole problem

00:03:23.577 --> 00:03:26.368
is built from solutions of subproblems

00:03:26.441 --> 00:03:27.690
of the same kind.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.300
A correct recursive definition

00:03:30.375 --> 00:03:31.806
has two properties.

00:03:31.806 --> 00:03:34.669
It has a base case, a stopping point

00:03:34.669 --> 00:03:37.606
and every allowed input must reach it.

00:03:37.682 --> 00:03:40.845
And each recursive call moves the argument

00:03:40.921 --> 00:03:43.557
from a higher value to a lower one

00:03:43.557 --> 00:03:45.440
closer to the base case.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.520
The slides illustrate this with

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:50.617
a function like this one.

00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:52.714
It does have a base case

00:03:52.714 --> 00:03:55.053
n <= 0 returns 0.

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.069
But look at the argument

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.054
of the recursive call:

00:04:00.054 --> 00:04:00.860
n / 3 + 1.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.423
Call f(1).

00:04:02.423 --> 00:04:06.237
1 / 3 is 0 in integer division

00:04:06.237 --> 00:04:09.617
so it calls f(1) again, and again

00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:10.570
and again.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.142
In fact n / 3 + 1 is at least 1

00:04:14.221 --> 00:04:17.129
for every n that is not negative

00:04:17.207 --> 00:04:19.565
so no call with n >= 1

00:04:19.644 --> 00:04:21.766
ever reaches the base case.

00:04:21.844 --> 00:04:23.966
Java gives up when the call

00:04:24.045 --> 00:04:26.009
stack runs out of memory

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:28.760
and throws a stack overflow error.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:32.591
The program prints f(0) = 0

00:04:32.591 --> 00:04:34.120
and then crashes.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.987
The base case exists

00:04:35.987 --> 00:04:38.110
but it is never reached.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.861
The fix is to stop at 1.

00:04:40.861 --> 00:04:42.531
For n at least 2

00:04:42.614 --> 00:04:45.369
n / 3 + 1 is strictly

00:04:45.453 --> 00:04:46.705
smaller than n

00:04:46.705 --> 00:04:49.210
so every chain of calls ends.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.897
Take f(10).

00:04:50.897 --> 00:04:54.907
It calls f(4), which calls f(2)

00:04:54.907 --> 00:04:58.440
which calls f(1): the base case.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.067
f(1) returns 0.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:05.310
f(2) adds 1, f(4) adds 3

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:09.139
giving 4, and f(10) adds 9

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:10.173
13.

00:05:10.173 --> 00:05:14.830
Each return pops a frame off the call stack.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.660
The program prints f(n)

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:20.041
for n from 0 to 10

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:22.620
and f(10) is 13.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.241
The Fibonacci numbers are

00:05:25.322 --> 00:05:28.260
defined recursively: F_0 is 0

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.770
F_1 is 1, and each next one is the sum

00:05:31.852 --> 00:05:33.566
of the 2 before it.

00:05:33.647 --> 00:05:35.688
The definition translates

00:05:35.769 --> 00:05:37.320
directly into Java.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.468
Here are the calls made

00:05:39.546 --> 00:05:41.188
by fibonacci(5).

00:05:41.188 --> 00:05:43.376
It calls fibonacci(4)

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:45.252
and fibonacci(3)

00:05:45.252 --> 00:05:47.910
and each of those calls 2 more.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.756
Look: fibonacci(3) is computed twice

00:05:51.756 --> 00:05:54.519
and fibonacci(2) 3 times.

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:56.552
The same subproblems are

00:05:56.633 --> 00:05:58.340
solved over and over.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.394
So the program is correct but slow.

00:06:01.471 --> 00:06:03.943
fibonacci(10) takes 177

00:06:04.021 --> 00:06:05.875
calls

00:06:05.952 --> 00:06:08.424
and fibonacci(40) more than

00:06:08.502 --> 00:06:10.201
300 million.

00:06:10.278 --> 00:06:12.596
The number of calls grows like

00:06:12.673 --> 00:06:14.450
1.6 to the n.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.112
The fix is memoization

00:06:17.203 --> 00:06:19.919
remember each answer the first

00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:23.087
time it is computed, and reuse it.

00:06:23.178 --> 00:06:25.260
An array memo holds the

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.251
answers found so far

00:06:27.251 --> 00:06:30.510
with 0 meaning not computed yet.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.529
If memo[n] is still zero

00:06:33.529 --> 00:06:36.495
compute it once and store it.

00:06:36.495 --> 00:06:39.460
Either way, return memo[n].

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.794
For fibonacci(10)

00:06:41.794 --> 00:06:44.628
the memo starts as all zeros

00:06:44.628 --> 00:06:47.556
and the calls run down to 2.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.012
Then the answers fill the

00:06:50.106 --> 00:06:53.979
memo from the bottom up: 1, 2, 3

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:57.286
5, and so on, up to 55.

00:06:57.286 --> 00:07:00.025
Each value is computed once

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:02.386
every later request is a

00:07:02.481 --> 00:07:04.370
single array lookup.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.109
Now fibonacci(10) takes 19 calls

00:07:08.191 --> 00:07:11.415
instead of 177.

00:07:11.415 --> 00:07:15.134
With a fresh memo, fibonacci(n) makes only

00:07:15.217 --> 00:07:18.440
2n - 1 calls, even for 90.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:21.494
Fibonacci is not divide-and-conquer

00:07:21.572 --> 00:07:23.426
in the merge-sort sense

00:07:23.426 --> 00:07:25.976
because its subproblems overlap.

00:07:26.053 --> 00:07:28.294
Memoization is the first step

00:07:28.371 --> 00:07:30.534
towards dynamic programming

00:07:30.534 --> 00:07:33.470
which you will meet in later courses.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:38.019
Many classic algorithms use divide-and-conquer

00:07:38.019 --> 00:07:40.215
merge sort and quicksort

00:07:40.215 --> 00:07:43.761
later in this lecture, and binary search

00:07:43.761 --> 00:07:45.450
from lecture three.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.988
Also fast exponentiation

00:07:48.076 --> 00:07:50.352
Euclid's algorithm for the

00:07:50.439 --> 00:07:52.540
greatest common divisor

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.954
Strassen's fast matrix multiplication

00:07:56.041 --> 00:07:59.105
and Karatsuba's fast multiplication

00:07:59.192 --> 00:08:00.680
of long integers.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.142
To sum up.

00:08:02.142 --> 00:08:05.581
One: a recursive function needs a base

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:08.314
case that every input reaches

00:08:08.314 --> 00:08:11.136
and calls that move towards it.

00:08:11.136 --> 00:08:14.046
Two: divide-and-conquer divides

00:08:14.046 --> 00:08:17.220
conquers recursively, and combines.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:20.557
Three: when subproblems overlap

00:08:20.557 --> 00:08:22.144
as in Fibonacci

00:08:22.144 --> 00:08:25.970
memoization computes each one only once.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.622
Try this yourself

00:08:27.695 --> 00:08:30.010
how many moves does the Tower of

00:08:30.082 --> 00:08:32.036
Hanoi take with four disks?

00:08:32.036 --> 00:08:34.640
And what are the first three moves?

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.938
In part four: merge sort

00:08:36.938 --> 00:08:39.975
divide-and-conquer applied to sorting

00:08:39.975 --> 00:08:42.390
and a first look at quicksort.

